[[File:Sefton Council Election Results Map 2022.svg|thumb|Results of the 2022 Sefton Metropolitan Borough Council election]]
The '''2022 Sefton Metropolitan Borough Council election''' took place on 5 May 2022 to elect members of [[Sefton Council]]. This was on the same day as [[2022 United Kingdom local elections|other local elections]]. 21 of the 66 seats were up for election, with 1 ward (St. Oswald) being uncontested.<ref>{{cite web |title=Election timetable in England |url=https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/election-timetable-in-england/election-timetable-in-england#metropolitan-districts |website=GOV.UK |access-date=7 April 2022 |language=en}}</ref><ref name="Oswald" />

==Background==
From its creation in 1974 to the 1986 election, Sefton Council was under [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative]] control.<ref>{{cite web |title=Sefton Council Election Results 1973-2012 |url=http://www.electionscentre.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2015/06/Sefton-1973-2012.pdf |website=Elections Centre |access-date=8 April 2022 |page=28 }}</ref> It was then under [[no overall control]] until the [[2012 Sefton Metropolitan Borough Council election|2012 election]], when [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] won their first majority on the council.<ref>{{cite news |title=BBC News - Vote 2012 - Sefton |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/vote2012/council/E08000014.stm |access-date=8 April 2022 |work=BBC News}}</ref> In the [[2021 Sefton Metropolitan Borough Council election|2021 election]], Labour polled 46.6% and gained 6 seats, the Conservatives polled 26.0% for a gain of 2 seats, The [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrats]] took 14.1% and lost 5 seats, Formby Residents Action Group polled 2.2% and did not make any gains, and independents lost their 3 seats on the council.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lally |first1=Kate |title=Labour make huge gains and boost majority in Sefton |url=https://www.liverpoolecho.co.uk/news/liverpool-news/local-elections-2021-labour-makes-20549627 |access-date=8 April 2022 |work=Liverpool Echo |date=7 May 2021 |language=en}}</ref>

The seats up for election this year were last elected in [[2018 Sefton Metropolitan Borough Council election|2018]]. In that election, Labour gained 3 seats with 50.9% of the vote, the Liberal Democrats lost 4 seats on 16.0%, and the Conservatives gained 1 seat on 22.8%. Formby Residents Action Group lost the seat they were defending.

